speechtext:
I have gathered from the remarks of the hon. members who spoke yesterday and the hon. member who has just taken his seat (Mr. Black) that there is opposition to the licenses granted to exporters in British Columbia,-they all seem to agree on that point; and further that there is really some bootlegging going on in British Columbia, and they all seem to be of opinion that a stop should be put to that traffic. The objection was offered yesterday by the hon. member for Vancouver Centre (Mr. Stevens) that the bill as we have introduced it was not in conformity with the resolution adopted by the legislature of British Columbia, and he added that it was not necessary to legislate to prohibit- the importation of intoxicating liquors because already the province had the right to do so. He cited the opinion of the Deputy Minister of Justice to that effect. Hon members will remember that yesterday in closing my remarks I said that if it was the wish of the hon. members for British Columbia that we should drop subsection (d), that is to say, make no exception in favour of the exporters, I was disposed to discuss that point. I would like to make my position in this matter as clear as possible. My predecessor, that is to say the hon. ex-Solicitor General, Mr. McKenzie, who drafted this bill and introduced it, had in his hands at the time a resolution which was adopted by the legislature of British Columbia in the month of December, 1922. That resolution reads as follows:
Whereas a plebiscite was taken in the province of British Columbia in October, 1920, on the following question:-
Which do you Prefer?
(1)	The present "Prohibition Act", or
(2)	An Act to provide for government control and sale in sealed packages of spirituous and mailt liquors?
And whereas the vote was strongly in favour of government control and sale:
And whereas as a result of the said plebiscite there was passed by the legislature of this province the "Government Liquor Act";
And whereas eighteen months off administration under that act has demonstrated the necessity for the prohibition of the importation of liquor into this province by other than the provincial government in order to accomplish the effective working of the said Act;
And whereas federal legislation to that end was sought at the meeting of the federal parliament in the spring of 1922, and the said proposed legislation met with the unanimous approval of the House of
Canada Temperance Act

Commons, but was defeated in the Senate at the olose of its session when many Senators were not present;
And whereas it is desirable that this province again seek the enactment of the proposed legislation;
Therefore be it resolved, that the federal parliament be requested at its next sittings to enact legislation providing for the prohibition of the importation of intoxicating liquor into British Columbia for use therein except by the provincial government; such legislation to be for the purpose of supplementing and giving effect to the aiforamemtioned "Government Liquor Act" and to be contingent upon and to exist only during its continuance;
And be it further resolved, that an humble address be presented to His Honour the Lieutenant-Governor, praying that he will cause a copy of the preamble and resolution hereinbefore set out to be transmitted to the Hon. the Secretary of State at Ottawa.
So, there was a plebiscite held in the province of British Columbia through which the electors by a very large majority declared themselves in favour of the sale of liquor by the government. Last year the government of British Columbia came to this parliament and asked that we should legislate to prohibit the importation of liquor. This bill of - ours was passed unanimously by this House, but defeated by the Senate. It is after that refusal of the Senate to accept such legislation that the legislature of British Columbia unanimously, I understand, by a large majority anyhow-
